      "text": "Nathan will roll two six-sided dice.\nTo find the probability that he rolls a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die,\nwe can consider the outcomes separately for each die.\nFor the first die, we have three possible outcomes (1, 2, 3) less than three, and three possible outcomes (4, 5, 6) greater than three.\nFor the second die, we have three possible outcomes (1, 2, 3) less than three, and three possible outcomes (4, 5, 6) greater than three.\nThus, the probability of both events happening is the product of the individual probabilities.\nThe probability of rolling a number less than three on the first die is $\\frac{3}{6}=\\frac{1}{2}$,\nand the probability of rolling a number greater than three on the second die is $\\frac{3}{6}=\\frac{1}{2}$.\nTherefore, the probability that Nathan will roll a number less than three on the first die and a number greater than three on the second die is $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot\\frac{1}{2}=\\boxed{\\frac{1}{4}}$.\nThe answer is: \\frac{1}{4}",
